  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Bitcoin's regulation bull case is played out. What could drive crypto gains moving forward",
      "source": "CNBC Tech",
      "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/20/bitcoin-price-catalysts-tokenization-regulation.html",
      "published_at": "2026-11-20T18:07:18+00:00",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-11-23T10:36:09.142817+00:00",
      "excerpt": "Crypto investors are hunting for the next positive catalyst after U.S. crypto regulation — a longtime market driver — fizzled in 2026.\nBitcoin traded around $61,500 on Friday, a more than 50% decline from its all-time high above $126,000 reached in October 2025. The broader digital asset market has struggled after the Clarity Act, a crypto market structure bill, failed to advance in the Senate in September.\nInvestors now see Wall Street's embrace of blockchain technology — particularly tokenization — as the next trend to give bitcoin a boost.\n\"Every single traditional firm is now looking at digital assets,\" BitGo CEO Mike Belshe told CNBC last week in an exclusive interview from the fintech-focused Cantor Convergence conference in Miami Beach. That includes BlackRock, the world's largest asset manager.\nHe pointed to statements from the asset management giant's CEO, Larry Fink, who said the next major evolution in global finance is the creation of digital representations of every stock, bond, fund and real-world asset onto a shared blockchain ledger.\n\"I think the biggest catalyst we're seeing right now is actually very much in motion and it's happening, which is capital markets are moving onchain,\" eToro CEO Yoni Assia told CNBC in an exclusive interview from the conference. He pointed to the SpaceX IPO as an example, which had derivatives trading on eToro before the IPO.\n\"First day of trading, we distributed it to our retail clients, and it started trading 24/7, and that's all because capital markets are now copying crypto markets and are sitting on the same rails,\" Assia said. \"Crypto markets have always been 24/7, they never sleep, and now what we're seeing is that capital markets are copying crypto markets using the same technology so stocks are moving onchain, they're being tokenized.\"\nAssia said that supportive regulation in the U.S. from agencies, including the Securities and Exchange Commission and the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, is also helping adoption of crypto.\nHe pointed to the CFTC earlier this month clearing the way for crypto-style perpetual futures tied to individual stocks to trade on regulated U.S. exchanges for the first time. Assia also highlighted the SEC's move to issue an order in September, creating a regulatory pathway for certain trading venues to trade tokenized representations of publicly traded U.S. stocks.\n\"Blockchain technology is now starting to power what's happening in capital markets,\" Assia told CNBC. \"That is the one of the biggest changes we're going to see in capital markets for many, many years.\"\n\"We're talking about hundreds of trillions of dollars that are going to be tokenized and move onchain and at the same time, I do believe that will support prices of bitcoin and other blockchains as well,\" he continued.\nThe industry is also watching whether inflation will ease and geopolitical tensions will subside in the new year, which could lower broader macro risk currently hampering the industry.\nLast",
